1
00:00:03,585 --> 00:00:05,740
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:05,741 --> 00:00:10,860
This time, let's learn about how to use Photoshop functions.

3
00:00:10,861 --> 00:00:14,261
You can think of it as a continuation of last time.

4
00:00:18,615 --> 00:00:23,120
First, let me go through the functions I often use, one by one.

5
00:00:23,121 --> 00:00:26,720
First, there's the blur effect function.

6
00:00:26,721 --> 00:00:32,521
There are various blur effect functions, but in concept art I don't use them much.

7
00:00:33,210 --> 00:00:37,100
The one I use most is this Gaussian blur effect.

8
00:00:37,101 --> 00:00:40,380
It's a simple function, so it's easy to use.

9
00:00:40,381 --> 00:00:45,020
You can blur the background to give a depth-of-field effect like this.

10
00:00:48,705 --> 00:00:52,700
Also, after duplicating an object like this, apply a Gaussian blur effect.

11
00:00:54,060 --> 00:01:01,260
And change the layer property.

12
00:01:03,470 --> 00:01:10,590
You can easily give a glow effect.

13
00:01:10,591 --> 00:01:12,350
And the motion blur effect too.

14
00:01:13,614 --> 00:01:16,680
It's a function I sometimes use.

15
00:01:16,681 --> 00:01:18,760
It gives a blur effect in a set direction.

16
00:01:29,474 --> 00:01:34,190
You can give an afterimage effect to something moving fast or a cape fluttering.

17
00:01:37,369 --> 00:01:43,340
The radial blur effect, I use occasionally too.

18
00:01:43,341 --> 00:01:45,340
If you actually use it, you get the feel for it.

19
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:54,051
It's similar to motion blur, but blurs radially around the center like this.

20
00:01:57,949 --> 00:02:01,840
It's good when you want to give the effect of light spreading out in all directions from one place.

21
00:02:06,649 --> 00:02:09,500
And there's the liquify that many people use.

22
00:02:11,070 --> 00:02:12,070
Let me take a look at it.

23
00:02:14,484 --> 00:02:18,041
Since many people use it, I'll briefly go over it.

24
00:02:21,390 --> 00:02:27,591
You can push things like this, or restore them.

25
00:02:31,095 --> 00:02:34,480
You can smooth out the parts you modified like this.

26
00:02:35,984 --> 00:02:39,450
And you can change them too.

27
00:02:39,451 --> 00:02:42,211
You can also rotate it like a vortex shape like this.

28
00:02:50,990 --> 00:02:58,990
You can make it concave or convex.

29
00:02:59,070 --> 00:03:03,790
This liquify is what I think of as Photoshop's secret weapon of a function.

30
00:03:03,791 --> 00:03:06,270
But this secret weapon is like a double-edged sword, you know.

31
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:10,750
Should I say that using the secret weapon also deals a blow to me?

32
00:03:10,751 --> 00:03:17,271
This function is so good and convenient that I've seen many cases of people making pictures with it.

33
00:03:19,670 --> 00:03:26,520
These Photoshop functions should be used only to the extent of giving a little help to my picture.

34
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:39,000
If you think you can't draw without this function, can't finish, or the result is terrible if it's absent, then you should think that you've been studying drawing wrong.

35
00:03:39,001 --> 00:03:44,520
If you think that as long as the result is good, that's fine, I won't stop you.

36
00:03:44,521 --> 00:03:53,241
Anyway, even when using these functions, you need to grasp the human form or the shape of the object well to use them accurately.

37
00:03:54,355 --> 00:04:04,540
If you fiddle around here and there without a firm idea of how to fix it, it's easy to lose the sense of whether it's really doing well or not.

38
00:04:04,541 --> 00:04:14,140
At least when studying, let's set aside this liquify for a while, and if there's a part of the picture that needs fixing, practice by erasing and redrawing it.

39
00:04:15,825 --> 00:04:19,529
I keep getting the urge to teach, which is a bit uncomfortable for me, too.

40
00:04:19,530 --> 00:04:20,089
What can I do?

41
00:04:20,090 --> 00:04:22,490
I'm the instructor, you know.

42
00:04:22,491 --> 00:04:26,131
Anyway, this liquify has a fun feature.

43
00:04:28,380 --> 00:04:36,350
If you draw a face properly, and thankfully Photoshop recognizes it as a face, there's a feature you can use.

44
00:04:36,430 --> 00:04:48,070
You can move eye size, eye level, width, tilt, and nose height like this, right?

45
00:04:49,980 --> 00:04:53,550
The width of the mouth, smile.

46
00:04:55,395 --> 00:04:56,140
It's really strange.

47
00:04:56,141 --> 00:05:04,460
Lips, lip width, height, and so on, you can adjust them in this much detail.

48
00:05:09,210 --> 00:05:10,940
I don't use it, but it's fun.

49
00:05:10,941 --> 00:05:14,341
It feels like customizing in a game, I'd say.

50
00:05:18,520 --> 00:05:22,650
I'd like to introduce a feature I've recently discovered and use a lot.

51
00:05:22,651 --> 00:05:25,210
It's this filter gallery.

52
00:05:25,370 --> 00:05:28,690
Students and aspiring artists, press the back button.

53
00:05:29,055 --> 00:05:33,850
If you use this at the completion stage of a picture, the filter shows a lot.

54
00:05:37,080 --> 00:05:45,050
But if you use it in the middle and retouch, you can bring in only the good feel of the filter and use it naturally in my own style.

55
00:05:46,395 --> 00:05:48,320
Let me click each one like this.

56
00:05:52,165 --> 00:05:53,520
How do these feel to you?

57
00:05:57,910 --> 00:06:03,800
For something like this, the look changes drastically, and the clothing takes on a metal feel.

58
00:06:03,801 --> 00:06:06,881
What I often use is this poster edges filter.

59
00:06:08,030 --> 00:06:12,000
Lines form at each boundary like this, making the picture very sharp.

60
00:06:14,040 --> 00:06:15,930
The contrast becomes very strong too.

61
00:06:15,931 --> 00:06:17,530
The picture looks a bit sturdier.

62
00:06:17,531 --> 00:06:20,010
It's a filter I use a lot these days.

63
00:06:20,011 --> 00:06:21,770
Rather than using it as-is.

64
00:06:30,390 --> 00:06:33,240
Duplicate the layer, apply the filter.

65
00:06:36,990 --> 00:06:44,361
Lower the opacity to about twenty percent, merge, and it can be applied subtly.

66
00:06:44,950 --> 00:06:47,000
From this state, continue drawing the picture.

67
00:06:48,285 --> 00:06:49,640
Let me see an example.

68
00:06:53,015 --> 00:06:58,780
After applying this filter gallery to this picture and lowering the opacity.

69
00:07:02,245 --> 00:07:04,880
Let me compare.

70
00:07:04,960 --> 00:07:09,440
A picture that had been drawn a bit blurry has become sharp and well defined.

71
00:07:09,441 --> 00:07:13,120
So I thought the feel got better.

72
00:07:13,121 --> 00:07:17,840
In this way, using the filter gallery to find a new feel is also fun.

73
00:07:21,795 --> 00:07:24,750
Just boil it a bit more like this and it'll be complete.

74
00:07:27,160 --> 00:07:30,410
There are various other functions too.

75
00:07:31,430 --> 00:07:32,730
Shall we go through them one by one?

76
00:07:41,390 --> 00:07:45,110
For something like this, bring out the brush-mark feel.

77
00:07:46,590 --> 00:07:49,040
And apply it.

78
00:07:49,041 --> 00:07:59,481
If I emphasize it once more, if you use the filter gallery like this, you must mix in my painting style through retouching.

79
00:08:01,445 --> 00:08:05,920
So it's good to use it in the early-to-mid stage rather than the final stage of a picture.

80
00:08:09,925 --> 00:08:17,200
We looked at Photoshop functions as seasoning that turns an eighty-point picture into an eighty-five.

81
00:08:18,465 --> 00:08:22,700
This is unrelated to the basic stuff, so please take it as a careful reference.

82
00:08:27,055 --> 00:08:31,160
Let's also learn about a method to increase the sharpness of a picture.

83
00:08:31,161 --> 00:08:36,840
If you go to the filter sharpen effect, there are various sharpen effect functions.

84
00:08:38,300 --> 00:08:46,670
You can use the function that the program automatically sharpens like this, but it's hard to create the sharpness I want.

85
00:08:47,990 --> 00:08:54,920
For the sharpness I want, I use this unsharp mask.

86
00:08:54,921 --> 00:09:11,720
To briefly explain the principle of sharpening a picture: the boundaries between objects with large value differences, that is, for example, the boundary between this hand and the coat, or the boundary between the coat and background, you could say.

87
00:09:11,721 --> 00:09:16,120
It increases the contrast of these boundaries to make them more distinct.

88
00:09:17,245 --> 00:09:20,840
Let me look at the unsharp mask.

89
00:09:20,841 --> 00:09:24,721
Here, this amount determines how much to increase the contrast.

90
00:09:26,620 --> 00:09:36,990
If you set it to a hundred percent, the dark pixels of the boundary are a hundred percent darker, and the bright pixels are a hundred percent brighter.

91
00:09:39,375 --> 00:09:42,120
Meaning it gives a hundred percent contrast.

92
00:09:43,565 --> 00:09:44,565
The radius is.

93
00:09:45,685 --> 00:09:49,230
It sets the radius of this boundary.

94
00:09:49,231 --> 00:09:54,590
That is, it determines how many pixels around the boundary to apply it to.

95
00:09:57,155 --> 00:10:01,870
If you raise this to the maximum, it creates the effect of adjusting the whole picture's levels.

96
00:10:04,405 --> 00:10:09,031
This threshold is the value that sets the tolerance of the boundary.

97
00:10:09,890 --> 00:10:17,281
A boundary with a value difference lower than the level value set here won't get this sharpen effect.

98
00:10:18,360 --> 00:10:24,330
The lower this value, the more the sharpen effect will apply to all parts that appear to be boundaries.

99
00:10:26,750 --> 00:10:40,640
Let's set aside such hard-to-understand content. I usually use an amount of about fifty to a hundred percent, a radius of one to five pixels, and a threshold of about one to ten levels.

100
00:10:41,955 --> 00:10:47,870
Adjusting it like this and comparing, you can see the picture gets a bit sharper.

101
00:10:51,190 --> 00:10:53,640
You can also do it another way.

102
00:10:53,641 --> 00:10:56,801
The method I'll introduce from now is better.

103
00:10:57,765 --> 00:11:02,320
Duplicate the picture, filter, other.

104
00:11:03,745 --> 00:11:05,180
Click high pass.

105
00:11:06,300 --> 00:11:12,220
Then adjust the radius value to the point where the outline is visible.

106
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:20,051
Press OK, then change the layer property to overlay.

107
00:11:21,795 --> 00:11:25,511
If you try it, you can see it gets sharper.

108
00:11:26,639 --> 00:11:32,240
Since it's been made into a layer, you can adjust the sharpness through opacity.

109
00:11:36,250 --> 00:11:41,461
Also, you can adjust it while changing the layer property.

110
00:11:48,615 --> 00:11:53,120
You can sharpen a pattern reference that isn't sharp, like this.

111
00:12:04,510 --> 00:12:09,550
When you draw concept art or illustration, you often have to draw patterns.

112
00:12:09,551 --> 00:12:13,550
Patterns often easily raise the density of a picture.

113
00:12:13,551 --> 00:12:16,711
There are various ways to insert patterns.

114
00:12:18,140 --> 00:12:26,750
There's the method of drawing directly onto the clothing, the method of compositing photo references, and the method of drawing a pattern and compositing it.

115
00:12:26,751 --> 00:12:32,510
The method of drawing directly onto the clothing was introduced in the clothing chapter when we drew the clothing's grid pattern.

116
00:12:33,285 --> 00:12:45,870
It's a method used when drawing simple patterns, and its advantage is that, compared to the other methods, it can be drawn naturally to match my art style and the clothing folds.

117
00:12:45,871 --> 00:12:50,830
The downside is that for complex patterns, you need huge time and effort.

118
00:12:53,125 --> 00:13:02,560
The method of compositing a photo is the fastest, but you have to find the pattern you want, and there are copyright issues too.

119
00:13:02,561 --> 00:13:06,640
Depending on the photo, you may need to make it into a pattern.

120
00:13:06,641 --> 00:13:14,240
The method of drawing a pattern and compositing it has the advantage of cutting time compared to directly drawing it on the clothing.

121
00:13:14,241 --> 00:13:17,520
You can draw the pattern you want.

122
00:13:17,521 --> 00:13:21,240
However, drawing the pattern takes considerable time.

123
00:13:21,605 --> 00:13:25,600
And if you can't composite it to match the folds, the naturalness somewhat decreases.

124
00:13:27,375 --> 00:13:30,440
Let me briefly go through each one.

125
00:13:30,441 --> 00:13:33,960
The method of drawing directly onto the clothing was explained in Chapter 5.

126
00:13:36,360 --> 00:13:42,680
I draw the pattern really honestly.

127
00:13:42,681 --> 00:13:43,960
Establish the big lines.

128
00:13:45,470 --> 00:13:47,760
Then go into the detail.

129
00:14:19,820 --> 00:14:24,720
The advantage of this is naturalness, but the downside is it takes too long.

130
00:14:32,865 --> 00:14:34,460
Here you also have to do the coloring.

131
00:14:37,580 --> 00:14:44,140
For photo composition, you bring in a pattern photo you want like this, correct it, then composite it.

132
00:14:44,141 --> 00:14:51,140
Usually pattern references only exist in part like this, so you need to make it into a pattern and widen it.

133
00:14:51,610 --> 00:14:53,740
Let me duplicate it once and widen it.

134
00:14:58,910 --> 00:15:03,440
All sorts of seams don't connect.

135
00:15:03,520 --> 00:15:06,480
Let me learn how to make them connect.

136
00:15:06,481 --> 00:15:09,520
First, you have to analyze this pattern.

137
00:15:09,521 --> 00:15:10,521
Here, at the very top.

138
00:15:12,865 --> 00:15:13,865
The cut-off part.

139
00:15:43,235 --> 00:15:47,301
Let me figure out where on the lower part this corresponds to.

140
00:15:48,890 --> 00:15:52,060
When I duplicate the layer and overlap them.

141
00:15:57,445 --> 00:16:02,041
I'll change the layer property to see it well.

142
00:16:04,485 --> 00:16:08,121
Placing it here, where the pattern overlaps and connects.

143
00:16:09,630 --> 00:16:10,630
It's here, right?

144
00:16:11,710 --> 00:16:13,440
Then, after holding Control.

145
00:16:15,095 --> 00:16:18,841
If you click the layer, the overlapping part gets selected.

146
00:16:20,975 --> 00:16:25,240
Turn off the layer, and you can see this selected part. I'll erase it here.

147
00:16:26,990 --> 00:16:33,070
And if you connect it, it naturally connects.

148
00:16:59,509 --> 00:17:00,510
For the vertical, do it the same way.

149
00:17:00,511 --> 00:17:02,590
This completes the pattern reference.

150
00:17:05,749 --> 00:17:09,079
After connecting it all the way, you just composite it.

151
00:17:15,069 --> 00:17:16,319
Shall we do one more?

152
00:17:16,320 --> 00:17:19,359
This time, let's try a bit harder reference.

153
00:17:19,360 --> 00:17:24,720
A pattern shot like this often doesn't connect when you put them together.

154
00:17:28,219 --> 00:17:35,070
There's this value difference too. The top is bright and the bottom is dark.

155
00:17:35,070 --> 00:17:39,630
After grasping the pattern, let's extract only the part we need.

156
00:17:41,269 --> 00:17:47,121
Looking at it, this and this are the same pattern.

157
00:17:48,594 --> 00:17:53,100
And this and this are also the same pattern.

158
00:17:54,964 --> 00:17:58,110
Well, this and this are also the same.

159
00:17:58,111 --> 00:18:02,110
Since a pattern is a repetition of the same design, that means.

160
00:18:15,909 --> 00:18:16,910
We only need about this much of the pattern.

161
00:18:16,911 --> 00:18:17,911
Cut this part out.

162
00:18:21,340 --> 00:18:23,790
Copy it, flip it, and paste it.

163
00:18:26,084 --> 00:18:29,710
Then a left-right identical pattern is complete, right?

164
00:18:29,711 --> 00:18:33,991
And here too, cut the middle the same way.

165
00:18:46,654 --> 00:18:51,130
And this, here and here are the same pattern.

166
00:18:51,374 --> 00:18:52,810
So we don't need half of it.

167
00:18:55,899 --> 00:19:00,350
The cut-off part here is around here.

168
00:19:01,420 --> 00:19:02,420
The flower shape.

169
00:19:03,504 --> 00:19:08,211
It's the same here below. I'll remove this part.

170
00:19:09,689 --> 00:19:12,780
This completes the pattern.

171
00:19:12,780 --> 00:19:16,620
Copy, flip again, and paste.

172
00:19:18,709 --> 00:19:24,840
If you connect this pattern, you can confirm it connects naturally.

173
00:19:31,524 --> 00:19:35,200
In this way, you can complete a pattern reference.

174
00:19:35,201 --> 00:19:38,880
Let me extract from an even harder photo.

175
00:19:38,881 --> 00:19:40,800
Rotate it to about ninety degrees for working.

176
00:19:52,514 --> 00:19:54,110
Check the cut-off part.

177
00:19:56,169 --> 00:19:57,550
I'll cut the top here.

178
00:20:02,429 --> 00:20:05,881
Looking at where this cut-off part corresponds below.

179
00:20:07,549 --> 00:20:09,520
It's around here.

180
00:20:09,521 --> 00:20:10,521
I'll cut here too.

181
00:20:10,960 --> 00:20:11,960
And.

182
00:20:13,584 --> 00:20:16,620
Cut here too.

183
00:20:16,621 --> 00:20:17,621
We don't need this here, right?

184
00:20:19,709 --> 00:20:24,160
And since this is left-right symmetric, I'll cut again based on here.

185
00:20:26,169 --> 00:20:30,821
Then in the end, only this part remains as what's needed.

186
00:20:32,739 --> 00:20:39,351
Right, let me duplicate only this and check once.

187
00:20:46,280 --> 00:20:51,960
You can see it connects naturally.

188
00:20:52,040 --> 00:20:53,400
For the part that doesn't connect.

189
00:20:55,799 --> 00:20:58,970
Erase it the same way.

190
00:21:01,149 --> 00:21:05,641
This way, it connects naturally.

191
00:21:08,484 --> 00:21:12,800
And if there's a value difference like this and it doesn't connect.

192
00:21:14,649 --> 00:21:18,060
You can just brighten it with the dodge tool.

193
00:21:38,309 --> 00:21:39,310
It connects naturally.

194
00:21:39,311 --> 00:21:42,151
This is how easily you can make a pattern reference.

195
00:21:43,239 --> 00:21:47,720
Now let me apply the pattern reference I made to the clothing.

196
00:21:47,721 --> 00:21:57,480
When inserting a pattern, to avoid breaking the inherent value and color of the clothing, I mainly use this high pass function.

197
00:21:59,029 --> 00:22:01,240
Click it.

198
00:22:01,241 --> 00:22:10,001
It strips away the pattern's color, leaving only the raised and recessed feel, so it can be applied more naturally.

199
00:22:15,100 --> 00:22:23,751
After correcting with the high pass function, composite it with a property like overlay.

200
00:22:38,014 --> 00:22:41,690
Soft light is fine too.

201
00:22:41,691 --> 00:22:45,210
These small continuous patterns aren't hard.

202
00:22:45,259 --> 00:22:47,610
Just paste it.

203
00:22:47,611 --> 00:22:50,890
In Chapter 6, part 1 I explained the card-section example.

204
00:22:51,614 --> 00:22:57,290
A free-spirited pattern like this doesn't show much misalignment even if it doesn't match the folds or perspective.

205
00:22:58,549 --> 00:23:03,320
So when drawing concept art, I sometimes just paste it flat like this.

206
00:23:04,919 --> 00:23:08,170
When drawing an illustration, it's a bit more complex.

207
00:23:08,171 --> 00:23:12,970
If you want to be ambitious, you draw these patterns one by one to match the folds.

208
00:23:14,594 --> 00:23:18,030
Or, draw a grid like this.

209
00:23:20,669 --> 00:23:23,760
There's also a method of inserting them one by one.

210
00:23:23,761 --> 00:23:25,600
Cut to match the grid shape.

211
00:23:30,709 --> 00:23:43,400
Cut using the lasso like this, then insert with the warp function.

212
00:24:03,204 --> 00:24:05,840
Doing it like this, you wonder what you're doing.

213
00:24:06,694 --> 00:24:10,560
So it's good to use the method appropriate to each occasion.

214
00:24:12,569 --> 00:24:19,020
When you need to draw directly on clothing or draw a pattern to make, the deliberation really grows.

215
00:24:19,021 --> 00:24:22,620
If the pattern is too complex, it's hard to draw in.

216
00:24:22,621 --> 00:24:30,860
Especially patterns that go on local areas, you can't even see what pattern it is anyway, so you wonder whether I should put in this much effort.

217
00:24:30,861 --> 00:24:34,380
When drawing weapons, you especially draw a lot of patterns.

218
00:24:34,381 --> 00:24:40,340
I want to introduce a method of drawing patterns that I've enjoyed using, that's easy, and effective.

219
00:24:41,609 --> 00:24:43,530
These are weapons I've drawn.

220
00:24:43,531 --> 00:24:52,250
There are various weapons like this, but if you look closely, you can see they use some of the same patterns.

221
00:24:54,649 --> 00:24:56,380
Let me try drawing this.

222
00:25:00,864 --> 00:25:03,580
First, let me draw it as a band shape.

223
00:25:14,320 --> 00:25:19,840
You can use it in the same place, like in a dream.

224
00:25:19,841 --> 00:25:21,280
Draw the big lines like this.

225
00:25:23,280 --> 00:25:30,560
Then split it.

226
00:25:30,560 --> 00:25:32,480
It's simple.

227
00:25:32,640 --> 00:25:34,320
You just keep splitting it.

228
00:25:34,479 --> 00:25:39,680
And since I'll use it as a repeating pattern, I'll cut out only the needed part.

229
00:26:07,240 --> 00:26:13,160
I'll only draw this part and connect it.

230
00:26:13,161 --> 00:26:17,720
If I duplicate and flip it, it'll split once more, right?

231
00:26:17,899 --> 00:26:19,800
Just keep subdividing it like this.

